WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.515
Environmental-justice engagement is not a presentation after the choices are made.

2
00:00:04.515 --> 00:00:13.930
It is a process that gives affected communities timely information, practical access, and a real opportunity to shape the analysis, alternatives, policies, and priorities.

3
00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:22.850
This lesson draws from California DOJ's Best Practices for Implementing SB one thousand and current Government Code section sixty-five thousand three hundred two.

4
00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:26.484
It is independent study, not legal advice or an official training.

5
00:00:26.484 --> 00:00:28.828
Start early enough for input to matter.

6
00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:31.832
Identify affected communities with community help.

7
00:00:31.832 --> 00:00:41.447
Budget for language access, disability access, childcare, transportation, multiple schedules, trusted messengers, and compensation where appropriate and lawful.

8
00:00:41.447 --> 00:00:47.456
Use in-person, telephone, paper, and digital methods rather than assuming one channel is sufficient.

9
00:00:47.456 --> 00:00:52.263
Explain what is open for change, which body decides, and what constraints apply.

10
00:00:52.263 --> 00:00:56.890
Avoid asking residents to repeat harmful experiences without a clear purpose.

11
00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:00.676
Protect privacy and give participants control over attribution.

12
00:01:00.676 --> 00:01:02.707
Build an engagement decision log.

13
00:01:02.707 --> 00:01:10.462
For each major theme, record the source, evidence, staff response, proposal change, unresolved issue, and next decision point.

14
00:01:10.462 --> 00:01:11.816
Preserve disagreement.

15
00:01:11.816 --> 00:01:16.433
A polished summary that erases minority views can create a false consensus.

16
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:18.218
Measure more than attendance.

17
00:01:18.218 --> 00:01:30.712
Ask whether participants represented affected places and experiences, whether materials were usable, whether knowledge changed the analysis, and whether people could see the result of their contribution.

18
00:01:30.712 --> 00:01:32.355
Torrance application segment.

19
00:01:32.355 --> 00:01:44.024
This local segment cites the City of Torrance notice for a February twenty-fifth, twenty twenty-five Land Use Element and Safety Element Update information booth, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six.

20
00:01:44.024 --> 00:01:51.048
The notice invited public input and stated that environmental-justice goals, policies, and objectives would be incorporated.

21
00:01:51.048 --> 00:01:55.013
For study, place that event within a hypothetical engagement timeline.

22
00:01:55.013 --> 00:02:03.454
Add earlier listening, accessible alternatives review, focused outreach to affected communities, and a published response log before formal hearings.

23
00:02:03.454 --> 00:02:06.683
Ask what evidence would show that input changed the plan.

24
00:02:06.683 --> 00:02:11.554
This is not an evaluation of the City's actual engagement record or legal sufficiency.

25
00:02:11.554 --> 00:02:18.805
A complete review would require all official notices, materials, participation records, draft changes, and current legal advice.

26
00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:27.351
Engagement is meaningful when it can alter the work, when barriers are addressed, and when government reports back with an accountable decision trail.

27
00:02:27.351 --> 00:02:32.707
The DOJ state-authored source is public-domain material within the documented rights boundary.

28
00:02:32.707 --> 00:02:36.468
Third-party content and local example-policy wording are excluded.

29
00:02:36.468 --> 00:02:37.949
No endorsement is implied.
